 # The Product Model
@@ -98,6 +113,94 @@ and SDD roots. `coverage.md` describes model breadth.
 Use [`businesslens view`](./cli-view.md) to browse the current model as a local
 report while editing.
 
+## Variations
+
+**A Variation is a currently supported alternative form of a resource, with an
+explicit account of when it applies.** Interfaces, Experiences, Screens,
+Entities, Capabilities, Journeys and Business Rules can have Variations. Each
+alternative remains a resource of that same type. Domains classify resources;
+the Product describes the whole; Scenarios already describe concrete behavior
+cases. None of those three carries Variation metadata.
+
+Create Variations when the Product supports distinct alternative behavior or
+contracts. Do not duplicate a resource for each configured value, ordinary
+Scenario outcome, or visual treatment. A configurable spending limit can remain
+one Rule referring to a setting. An older API or record format still used by
+current clients or data is live Product meaning; an unsupported past definition
+belongs in Git history.
+
+| Subtype | Why alternatives coexist | What When used must explain |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `experiment` | Alternatives evaluate different outcomes, including A/B tests | Eligibility, assignment unit/condition, allocation where promised, and how long assignment stays stable |
+| `configuration` | A setting or operating context selects the behavior | Effective settings/context, defaults, overrides, and when changes take effect |
+| `version` | Distinct contracts or forms remain supported together | Version selection, omitted/unsupported choices, and behavior of existing clients or records |
+
+Classify the Product meaning, not all its implementation mechanisms. API v1/v2
+are Version even if a tenant setting selects one. An experiment enabled by a
+setting is Experiment. A permanent customer preference between workflows is
+Configuration. Explain combined mechanisms in When used; a set has one subtype.
+
+The set's anchor declares `variationKind`. Other members point to it with
+`variantOf`; they derive the subtype and do not repeat it. The anchor is an
+ordinary resource, not an implicit default or parent. Both files below retain
+their normal required fields and sections:
+
+```yaml
+# capabilities/standard-checkout/capability.md — anchor
+variationKind: configuration
+```
+
+```md
+## When used
+
+Used when the merchant's checkout mode is Standard or unset. The mode is read
+when checkout starts and retained until checkout ends.
+```
+
+```yaml
+# capabilities/guided-checkout/capability.md
+variantOf: standard-checkout
+```
+
+```md
+## When used
+
+Used when the merchant's checkout mode is Guided. The mode is read when checkout
+starts and retained until checkout ends.
+```
+
+Every member has its own **When used**, including the anchor. This section owns
+applicability; a separate selection Rule does not replace it. Across the set,
+explain missing selections, overlaps and changes during use wherever possible.
+Do not invent allocation percentages, defaults or precedence. Unknown selection
+behavior remains a gap to resolve through source evidence or approved intent.
+
+Relationships use exact resource ids: place ids are fully qualified, such as
+`reader-mobile::personal-library`. Links may cross containers, but never types.
+The link transfers no content, ownership, availability, navigation or permission.
+Each alternative is complete; ordinary references still name exact resources.
+There is no automatic selection or pairing of a Variation's children.
+
+For a **Business Rule Variation**, When used determines whether the Rule applies;
+its constraints and grants govern only then. Grant `when` still conditions an
+Entity operation. It cannot select a whole Rule. Lint checks reference/shape
+validity and warns that permission applicability in prose requires verification;
+it does not apply conditional permission Rules unconditionally to Steps or
+Screens. Verification must check selection and the applicable constraints.
+
+Lint rejects missing or cross-type targets, self-links, chains and cycles. The
+anchor needs one valid subtype and an incoming link; linked members cannot
+declare a subtype. All members need one non-empty When used section. A resource
+outside a set cannot carry When used or subtype metadata. Product, Domain and
+Scenarios cannot carry these fields or the section. Removing the last alternative
+also removes the survivor's Variation metadata; preserve still-relevant conditions
+in its ordinary meaning.
+
+Structural checks do not prove selection exhaustive, detect all conflicting
+prose conditions, or establish deterministic resource granularity. The model
+states Product behavior; it does not execute experiments, configuration or
+version negotiation.
+
 ## Is this replacing my PRD?
 
 No — they have different jobs, and only one section overlaps.
