+## Variations
+
+A Variation is a currently supported alternative form of a resource, with an
+explicit account of when it applies. Interfaces, Experiences, Screens, Entities,
+Capabilities, Journeys and Business Rules support it. Product, Domain and both
+Scenario types do not. Scenarios remain concrete cases of their parent behavior.
+This supersedes the Experience-only, same-Interface variation contract.
+
+The format has one encoding:
+
+- The anchor declares `variationKind: experiment | configuration | version`.
+  It has no `variantOf` and must have at least one incoming variation link.
+- Every other member declares `variantOf: <id>` naming that anchor in the same
+  resource collection. Place ids are fully qualified. Members do not repeat
+  `variationKind`; their subtype derives from the anchor.
+- Every member, including the anchor, has one non-empty `## When used` section
+  of Markdown prose. It owns that member's applicability. It is required for
+  a variation member and forbidden on resources outside a variation set.
+- Targets must exist and differ from their source. Chains, cycles, cross-type
+  links, subtype declarations on linked members and orphan metadata are errors.
+  Peer lists and the subtype of a linked member are derived, never authored twice.
+
+```yaml
+# capabilities/standard-checkout/capability.md (anchor)
+variationKind: configuration
+```
+
+```yaml
+# capabilities/guided-checkout/capability.md
+variantOf: standard-checkout
+```
+
+Both files also carry their ordinary required fields, complete meaning and a
+`## When used`: for example, Standard applies when the merchant's checkout mode
+is Standard or unset; Guided applies when it is Guided. Both state that the mode
+is selected at checkout start and retained until checkout ends.
+
+The subtype names the Product meaning of the alternatives, not every mechanism
+used to select them:
+
+| Subtype | Meaning | What When used explains |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `experiment` | Alternatives offered to evaluate different outcomes | Eligibility, assignment unit and condition, allocation where promised, and assignment stability |
+| `configuration` | Alternatives selected through a setting or operating context | The effective setting/context, defaults and overrides, and when changes take effect |
+| `version` | Distinct contracts or forms supported at the same time | Version selection, omitted/unsupported selections, and behavior of existing clients or records |
+
+Version alternatives selected through a setting remain Version; an experiment
+enabled through configuration remains Experiment. A permanent customer preference
+between workflows is Configuration. Explain combined selection mechanisms in
+When used. Across the set, state missing-selection and overlap behavior where
+possible, and the duration of a selection where relevant. Do not invent defaults,
+allocation percentages, priority or evidence; unresolved meaning remains a gap.
+The rubric asks these questions; structural lint cannot prove prose exhaustive
+or establish deterministic classification or resource granularity.
+
+The anchor is not a default, parent, older release or source of inherited
+content. All members are current and independently complete. Links never create
+availability, navigation, permissions, transitions or paired children. All
+ordinary references continue to name exact resources; nothing auto-selects a
